23:14:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s actually based on an old misconception! The classic “tongue map” (sweet at the tip, bitter at the back) came from a misinterpretation of a 1901 German study and was popularized in textbooks for decades, but modern science has shown it’s wrong. In reality, **all parts of the tongue that contain taste buds can detect sweet, bitter, sour, salty, and umami**. Taste buds are located in small bumps called papillae all over the tongue, and each taste bud contains a mix of receptor cells responsive to different tastes. While there might be very slight regional differences in sensitivity (for example, some people may be a bit more sensitive to bitter at the back), the differences are tiny and not nearly as segregated as the old map suggested. So you taste sweet and bitter—and everything else—across the whole tongue.
